courses.learning_outcomes:
• To understand the historical development of global enterprises;

• To gain an appreciation for the complexities of the international challenges facing global enterprises;

• To discuss strategies for sequencing the penetration of countries and committing resources;

• To examine the major variables a global enterprise should consider when deciding whether and where to expand its operation;

• To explore international aspects of management (e.g. functions and strategies) in business operations

Upon completion of this course, students should be able to:
1. Understand the roles of social entrepreneurship in society, and the challenges and opportunities of social innovation;
2. Formulate innovative ways to identify and address social problems;
3. Design appropriate and accountable measures to evaluate social impacts;
4. Apply tools and frameworks for launching and managing social entrepreneurship;
5. Evaluate different financial and strategic options for social entrepreneurship.

Upon completion of this course, students should be able to:
1. Deliver innovative and feasible solutions in a real entrepreneurial context;
2. Accumulate hands-on working experience under guidance of practitioners;
3. Derive understanding of entrepreneurship-related knowledge and transform it through real entrepreneurship experience;
4. Develop analytical and problem-solving skills in a team with classmates and/or practitioners.

Upon completion of this course, students should be able to:
1. Acquire an in-depth understanding in one or more specific sectors in entrepreneurship;
2. Appreciate and evaluate the best practices, opportunities, and challenges in different contexts of entrepreneurship;
3. Employ tools and knowledge in entrepreneurship in analyzing real cases and generating solutions;
4. Develop problem-solving and communication skills by delivering convincing solutions.

After completing this course, students should be able to:
(1) understand HRM issues and practices which affect organizational performance;
(2) develop a strategic approach for managing human resources.

After completing this course, students should be able to:
(1) explain conceptual frameworks for analysing the complexity of management decision making;
(2) develop a holistic mind-set to study management decision making as an interaction of self, others and context;
(3) apply conceptual frameworks, effective decision-making skills and tactics to diagnose the practical management problems in the workplace and come up with proper solutions to deal with these problems; and
(4) create an environment in which students can enhance self-awareness and self-reflection, synthesize new ideas, and develop their personal strengths and decision-making abilities.

Students will be able to understand:
(1) programme strategy against grand strategy;
(2) the relations between business strategy and the different aspects of a business.
